// The geomath package: the numbers 2-D and 3-D worlds are built from. A
// program reaches it with 'import geomath', and gets value types:
//
// - Vectors: Vec2, Vec3 and Vec4, a point or a direction (and Vec2 a texture
//   coordinate), with the operators, dot and cross products, lengths and
//   distances, 'normalize', component-wise min, max, abs and clamp, lerp,
//   reflect, project and the angle between two; DVec3, a 64-bit position for
//   a world too large for f32, converted to and from Vec3.
// - Rotation and placement: Quat, an orientation; Mat3, a 3x3 matrix (a
//   rotation and scale, a normal matrix, or a 2-D placement); Mat4, a 4x4
//   matrix (projections, look-at, placements); Transform3 (translation,
//   rotation, scale) and Transform2 (translation, angle, scale).
// - Shapes: Aabb2 and Aabb3 (axis-aligned boxes), Rect (f32 corner and size)
//   and IRect (whole pixels), Circle and Sphere, Plane, Ray2 and Ray3, and
//   Frustum; with the tests between them: ray against box, sphere, plane and
//   triangle with the distance to the hit, box and sphere against a frustum,
//   box against box, point in box, closest points, and in 2-D ray and segment
//   intersections, point in rect and point in polygon.
// - Curves and polygons: quadratic and cubic Bezier curves in 2-D and 3-D,
//   polylines, and 2-D polygons' signed area and winding.
// - Color: red, green, blue and alpha.
//
// Each is a small struct passed by value: a method returns a new value and
// never changes the one it is called on. Numbers are f32, but for DVec3.
//
// Its conventions are the industry's:
//
// - Space is right-handed: x to the right, y up, z towards the viewer, so a
//   camera looks down its own -z. A positive angle turns counterclockwise when
//   seen from the tip of the axis looking back: a quarter turn about z takes x
//   to y. In 2-D, a positive angle turns x towards y.
// - Angles are radians everywhere, a camera's field of view included.
//   'radians' and 'degrees' convert.
// - A matrix is stored column by column (column-major): Mat4's m[0..3] is its
//   first column, and its translation is m[12], m[13], m[14]. 'a * b' applies
//   b first, then a, and 'm * p' transforms the point p (its w taken as 1).
// - After projection, depth runs from 0 at the near plane to 1 at the far one,
//   as Vulkan, Direct3D, Metal and WebGPU want it: Mat4.perspective,
//   Mat4.orthographic and Frustum.fromMatrix. OpenGL's -1 to 1 is
//   Mat4.perspectiveGl, Mat4.orthographicGl and Frustum.fromMatrixGl.
// - A quaternion is (x, y, z, w), w the scalar part; a rotation is a unit
//   quaternion, and 'a * b' is the rotation b followed by a.
// - A plane is the points p where normal.dot(p) + d = 0; its normal points to
//   its positive side, which for a frustum's planes is the inside.
// - A ray's hit is an Option[f32], the multiple of its direction at which it
//   hits: with a unit direction, the distance. A ray starting inside a solid
//   (a box or a sphere) hits it at 0.
//
// Every sine, cosine, tangent and inverse of one in this package goes through
// its own functions in scalar.cone ('geomath.sin', 'geomath.cos', ...), so that
// deterministic versions can later be put in, in one place. For now they are
// the f32 methods the compiler builds in and the libc package's; square roots
// are the compiler's.
//
// It is pure maths: values and the operations on them, each result of a size
// known in advance, so all of it can run on a GPU. It makes no list: a list
// of points it reads is a slice, so a fixed array ('&points') and a
// collections List ('list.view()') both serve. What makes geometry of a size
// not known in advance lives beside its users: a Bezier curve sampled into a
// polyline ('sampleCurve') and the convex hull ('convexHull') in sculpt, a
// polygon's triangulation ('triangulatePolygon') in mesh.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   scalar.cone     pi, radians and degrees, the trigonometry, scalar helpers
//   color.cone      Color
//   vector.cone     Vec2, Vec3, Vec4 and DVec3
//   quat.cone       Quat
//   matrix.cone     Mat3 and Mat4
//   transform.cone  Transform3 and Transform2
//   shape.cone      Aabb3, Aabb2, Rect, IRect, Circle, Sphere, Plane, Frustum
//   ray.cone        Ray3 and Ray2, segments, closest points, barycentric
//   curve.cone      Bezier curves and polylines
//   polygon.cone    signed area, winding, point in polygon
//
// It began as a port of the Pegasus3D browser's world libraries (xyz, quat,
// matrix4, xyzmath, color and rect), whose names and conventions were
// OpenGL's; where it differs from them now it follows the industry.

mod geomath;

import libc;
